  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe provided code uses SLSQP and coordinate descent to minimize the maximum Fourier transform magnitude. While effective, it can be improved in several ways. The central idea is to:\n   - Employ a two-stage optimization: coarse-grained phase (gradient descent) followed by fine-grained phase (coordinate-wise optimization)\n   - Incorporate multiple frequency optimization using harmonic analysis\n   - Adapt the optimization hyperparameters spatially\n   - Maintain the core functionality while significantly improving performance\n\nKey limitations are the fixed optimization parameters and the handling of the two-stage Fourier transform. This implementation addresses these by:\n   - Using harmonic expansion for faster optimization\n   - Switching between magnitude and phase optimization\n   - Implementing spatial adaptation of the penalty parameter\n   - Using iterative refinement with decreasing noise\n\nThe algorithm balances exploration and exploitation by combining gradient information with coordinate-wise fine-tuning.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization minimizes:\n   max_k |F{h}(k)|   subject to \u2211h_i=1 and 0\u2264h_i\u22641\n\nExpanded form:\n   C = max(|H(0)|, |H(1)|, ..., |H(n-1)|)   [operations over the data sequence]\n\nConstraints are enforced via:\n   (1) Normalization: normalize h to sum to 1\n   (2) Bound enforcement: project using penalty term\n\nThe objective transforms through:\n   C = max_k |\u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] * \u03c9^{i*k}/\u221an|   (for discrete Fourier)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nPhase 1: Initialization\n   - Initialize h randomly but with unit sum (n=500)\n   - Add white noise to enhance exploration\n\nPhase 2: Coarse optimization (gradient descent)\n   - Use Fourier transform of h as input\n   - Optimize on expanded frequency plane\n   - Handle high frequencies with post-processing\n   - Update rule: h = h + \u03b7*(grad - \u03bc*proj_bound)\n   - Clip values after updates\n\nPhase 3: Fine optimization (coordinate-wise)\n   - Synchronize h between time and frequency domains\n   - Optimize using coordinate descent with adaptive \u03bc\n   - Apply momentum to stable modes\n   - Use backtracking line search for \u03b7\n\nPhase 4: Post-processing\n   - Project to [0,1] ensuring sum=1\n   - Final Fourier transform to verify\n   - Clean up results and return\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Default parameters: drop_prob=0.1, \u03b7_start=0.1, tol=1e-10\n- Adaptive phase selection: estimate optimal number of iterations\n- Early termination: use inline check of improvement rate\n- Alternate initialization: start with random plus shifted Fourier components\n\nBudget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: Fourier transforms (O(n log n))\n- Time budget: ~1000 seconds for n=500\n- Warm-up: perform initial frequency analysis\n- Line search: use backtracking but limit to 10 attempts\n- Projection: quick check rather than full resampling\n\nValidation and return\n- Return (h_values, C5_value, n=500)\n- First check: sum(h_values)\u22481 (within 1e-5)\n- Second check: all h_values between 0 and 1\n- Final check: Fourier transform computation\n\nThe candidate must be optimized using both time-domain and frequency-domain techniques.",
